From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 12:18:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] payload: bound compressed sources and retain stream consumption

Separate a finite compressed-source capacity from the parser's object-size
argument. Known object sizes must fit the decoder's 32-bit count and have
a non-wrapping interval. Inline size-zero objects use a checked declared
top_of_kernel_data boundary, not an invented file length or linker capacity.
Reject overlapping source/output intervals before decoding and require a
positive consumed length within the original capacity before finalization.

XZ's documented API promises consumed input length, but previously retained
a nonzero supplied capacity. Return BfTell() after decoding so a finite bound
does not skip a following DTB/initrd. Preserve size-zero terminal Image and
other parser object-size semantics; no package format is changed.

gzip's existing APIs require exact stream lengths. Add explicit bounded
prefix APIs for gzip/raw deflate and use them only from the payload wrapper;
locate and validate the actual footer instead of reading the capacity end.
Keep the existing strict/legacy entry points and proxy behavior unchanged.

The handoff boundary and known lengths still require trustworthy backing.
This does not bound the complete dispatcher/variable parser, qualify initrd
placement, or establish physical mapping/cache/DMA/native-entry safety.
Existing XZ forward-seek overflow and intentional unsigned rewind semantics
also remain a separate hardening gate; no universal decoder-read proof.

AI assistance: OpenAI Codex; local Debian/project-fork work only. No Asahi
submission, hardware access, firmware, disk or VM operation.

